{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "כי תבוא", :translit "ki tavo", :gloss "when you come"} {:he "בימי שמואל", :translit "bimei shmuel", :gloss "in the days of Samuel"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :gematria, :a "כי תבוא", :b "בימי שמואל"}], :frame {:text "The Israelites' request for a king occurred in the days of Samuel, who anointed Saul as king (I Samuel 8:5, I Samuel 10:1). The gematria linking 'when you come' to 'in the days of Samuel' signals that the fulfillment of the verse's prediction about wanting a king like the nations came to pass specifically then.", :sources ["I Samuel 8:5" "I Samuel 10:1"]}} {:quotes [{:he "וישבתה", :translit "viyashavta", :gloss "and you shall dwell"} {:he "ה' דורות", :translit "hadorot", :gloss "five generations"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "The plene spelling of וישבתה with an extra heh (ה) signals five (ה=5), corresponding to five generations."}], :frame {:text "The Ba'al HaTurim reads the unusually plene spelling of וישבתה, which contains an extra letter heh (numerical value 5), as hinting that five generations after entering the land the Israelites would ask for a king. Counting from the conquest under Joshua: Joshua, the elders, then the era of the judges leading to Samuel spans this period (Judges 2:7–8 traces Joshua and the elders; I Samuel 8:5 records the request for a king in Samuel's time).", :sources ["Judges 2:7-8" "I Samuel 8:5"]}} {:quotes [{:he "ככל הגוים אשר סביבותי", :translit "kekhol ha-goyim asher sevivotai", :gloss "like all the nations that are around me"} {:he "סרנים", :translit "seranim", :gloss "lords/rulers"} {:he "אחר פרשת עכו\"ם ואשרה כתיב פרשת מלך", :translit "achar parshat akum va'asherah katuv parshat melekh", :gloss "after the section of idolatry and Asherah is written the section of the king"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Sequential placement of parashat melekh after idolatry and Asherah sections teaches the king's duty to eradicate them."} {:kind :other, :note "Philistine use of סרנים rather than מלך shows they had no king, motivating Israel's request to be 'like all the nations.'"}], :frame {:text "The Philistine rulers are consistently called סרנים rather than kings (Judges 3:3, I Samuel 5:8). The Torah's placement of the king section immediately after the idolatry section (Deuteronomy 16:21–17:7 precedes 17:14ff.) teaches the king's responsibility to combat idolatry, as the king's central task is to establish the Torah's authority (Deuteronomy 17:18–19).", :sources ["Judges 3:3" "I Samuel 5:8" "Deuteronomy 16:21-17:7" "Deuteronomy 17:18-19"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :gematria, :a "כי תבוא", :a-value 439, :b "בימי שמואל", :b-value 439, :lens :standard, :by :selah, :equal? true} {:kind :other, :note "The plene spelling of וישבתה with an extra heh (ה) signals five (ה=5), corresponding to five generations.", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "Sequential placement of parashat melekh after idolatry and Asherah sections teaches the king's duty to eradicate them.", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "Philistine use of סרנים rather than מלך shows they had no king, motivating Israel's request to be 'like all the nations.'", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:37:14.511277711Z", :model "glm-5.2"}